The older address-feature reports concern missing support, non-HTTP bindings, or casing, rather than first-request publication timing.

### Describe the bug

`IISHttpServer.StartAsync` registers native callbacks before assigning `_serverAddressesFeature.Addresses` from `_options.ServerAddresses`:

```csharp
_nativeApplication.RegisterCallbacks(/* ... */);

_serverAddressesFeature.Addresses = _options.ServerAddresses;
```

Native callback registration enables requests and signals the ANCM initialization event. A real IIS request can therefore reach application middleware before the address-feature assignment executes. Middleware inspecting `IServerAddressesFeature.Addresses` sees an empty collection, even though IIS bindings have already been read and stored in the options. The next request sees the expected bindings.

This is an **initialization/publication ordering issue**, not a late native configuration read. It affects applications that inspect or cache server addresses during their first request. It does not, by itself, cause the PathBase routing failure.

Source: [IISHttpServer.StartAsync](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/e523b3b676b8784e0bc8d6b1a574e5335c4ff3e5/src/Servers/IIS/IIS/src/Core/IISHttpServer.cs#L95-L116). The feature initially exposes `Array.Empty<string>()` in [ServerAddressesFeature](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/e523b3b676b8784e0bc8d6b1a574e5335c4ff3e5/src/Servers/IIS/IIS/src/Core/ServerAddressesFeature.cs).

### Expected Behavior

Before native callbacks can dispatch a request into managed code, the server should publish the IIS bindings through `IServerAddressesFeature.Addresses`. The startup-triggering request should observe the same bindings as subsequent requests.

### Steps To Reproduce

#### Setup and observation

1. On Windows, publish an ASP.NET Core application for **IIS in-process** hosting. Configure a site with an HTTP binding and an IIS application at `/myapi`. The tested environment used x64 IIS Express, locally built ANCM, and the installed ASP.NET Core 10.0.12 and 9.0.20 shared frameworks.
2. In middleware before routing, obtain `IServer` from DI and inspect:

   ```csharp
   var addresses = server.Features.Get<IServerAddressesFeature>()!.Addresses;
   ```

   Report its count and contents in response headers. Use middleware rather than just a routed endpoint: on 10.0, the separate PathBase issue can prevent the first request from reaching the endpoint.
3. Start a **fresh IIS Express process** with no HTTP warm-up request. Send `GET /myapi/v1/ping` twice.
4. A request that runs between callback registration and address publication reports count `0`; the next request reports the configured binding. This window is narrow, so an ordinary cold-start loop is not a reliable regression test.

#### Deterministic diagnostic used to reproduce the window

A small test-only native gate makes the real interleaving deterministic **without changing shipping server code**. It intercepts only the managed `register_callbacks` import, calls the **real ANCM registration function**, and waits until the first real request reaches the observation middleware before returning. It does not invoke request callbacks itself, replace `IServer`, or overwrite server state.

Save the following as `TimingGate.c` and compile it for the same architecture as IIS Express. In an x64 Visual Studio Native Tools command prompt:

```text
cl /nologo /LD /W3 TimingGate.c /link /OUT:TimingGate.dll
```

<details>
<summary>TimingGate.c</summary>

```c
#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef HRESULT (__cdecl *register_fn)(
    void*, void*, void*, void*, void*, void*, void*, void*);

static volatile LONG gate_state;

__declspec(dllexport) int __cdecl get_gate_state(void)
{
    return (int)InterlockedCompareExchange(&gate_state, 0, 0);
}

__declspec(dllexport) HRESULT __cdecl register_callbacks(
    void* application, void* request, void* shutdown, void* disconnect,
    void* async_completion, void* drained,
    void* request_context, void* shutdown_context)
{
    HMODULE original = GetModuleHandleW(L"aspnetcorev2_inprocess.dll");
    register_fn real_register =
        (register_fn)GetProcAddress(original, "register_callbacks");
    if (!real_register)
    {
        fprintf(stderr, "Original register_callbacks not found: %lu\n", GetLastError());
        return E_FAIL;
    }

    HRESULT result = real_register(application, request, shutdown, disconnect,
        async_completion, drained, request_context, shutdown_context);
    if (FAILED(result))
    {
        return result;
    }

    InterlockedExchange(&gate_state, 1);
    wchar_t name[100];
    swprintf_s(name, 100, L"Local\\IISPathBaseRepro-%lu", GetCurrentProcessId());
    HANDLE observed = OpenEventW(SYNCHRONIZE, FALSE, name);
    if (!observed)
    {
        fprintf(stderr, "Observation event missing: %lu\n", GetLastError());
        return E_FAIL;
    }

    DWORD wait = WaitForSingleObject(observed, 20000);
    CloseHandle(observed);
    if (wait != WAIT_OBJECT_0)
    {
        fprintf(stderr, "Request not observed before release: %lu\n", wait);
        return E_FAIL;
    }

    InterlockedExchange(&gate_state, 2);
    return result;
}
```

</details>

Copy `TimingGate.dll` beside the published application DLL. Use this diagnostic `Program.cs` (Web SDK, with implicit usings enabled):

<details>
<summary>Diagnostic application</summary>

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Server;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Server.Features;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

EventWaitHandle? requestObserved = null;
if (Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("IIS_REPRO_GATE") == "1")
{
    requestObserved = new EventWaitHandle(false, EventResetMode.ManualReset,
        $"Local\\IISPathBaseRepro-{Environment.ProcessId}");
    NativeLibrary.SetDllImportResolver(typeof(IISServerOptions).Assembly, (name, assembly, path) =>
    {
        var frames = new System.Diagnostics.StackTrace().GetFrames();
        if (name == "aspnetcorev2_inprocess.dll" && frames.Any(frame =>
            frame.GetMethod()?.Name == "StartAsync" || frame.GetMethod()?.Name == "HttpRegisterCallbacks"))
        {
            return NativeLibrary.Load(Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "TimingGate.dll"));
        }

        return IntPtr.Zero;
    });
}

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();
var server = app.Services.GetRequiredService<IServer>();
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    var addresses = server.Features.Get<IServerAddressesFeature>()!.Addresses;
    context.Response.Headers["X-Observed-AddressCount"] = addresses.Count.ToString();
    context.Response.Headers["X-Observed-Addresses"] = $"'{string.Join(';', addresses)}'";
    context.Response.Headers["X-Observed-Runtime"] =
        Path.GetFileName(Path.GetDirectoryName(server.GetType().Assembly.Location));
    if (requestObserved is not null)
    {
        context.Response.Headers["X-Observed-GateState"] = NativeDiagnostic.GetGateState().ToString();
    }

    requestObserved?.Set();
    await next(context);
});
app.MapGet("/v1/ping", () => "pong");
app.Run();

internal static class NativeDiagnostic
{
    [DllImport("TimingGate.dll", EntryPoint = "get_gate_state")]
    internal static extern int GetGateState();
}
```

The resolver's stack check is diagnostic-only. It selects the proxy at the callback-registration import while leaving all other imports bound to the real ANCM module.

</details>

For a standalone app, publish using the Web SDK, for example:

```powershell
dotnet publish .\IisRepro.csproj -f net10.0 -o .\published --no-self-contained -p:UseAppHost=false
```

Configure the published `web.config` for `hostingModel="inprocess"` and set the worker environment variable `IIS_REPRO_GATE=1`, for example under the `aspNetCore` element:

```xml
<environmentVariables>
  <environmentVariable name="IIS_REPRO_GATE" value="1" />
</environmentVariables>
```

Use the published app at `/myapi` in the IIS Express site, then launch a new worker:

```powershell
& "${env:ProgramFiles}\IIS Express\iisexpress.exe" /config:<absolute-applicationhost.config-path> /site:<site-name> /systray:false
```

From another terminal, make the first HTTP request directly to the test route (do not first request `/`, a readiness endpoint, or any other application URL):

```powershell
curl.exe -i http://localhost:<port>/myapi/v1/ping
curl.exe -i http://localhost:<port>/myapi/v1/ping
```

The first response must have `X-Observed-GateState: 1`; otherwise that run did not exercise the held registration boundary and is not evidence for the race. The second response has gate state `2`.

#### Observed results

Each row below represents three independently restarted IIS Express processes. All first-request observations occurred at native gate state `1`.

| ASP.NET Core runtime | First request | Second request |
|---|---|---|
| 10.0.12 | `AddressCount=0`, empty addresses | `AddressCount=1`, configured binding |
| 9.0.20 | `AddressCount=0`, empty addresses | `AddressCount=1`, configured binding |

Example binding on the second request: `http://localhost:51934/MYAPI`. The casing of the virtual path is native IIS behavior and is not part of this report.

On 9.0.20, both requests routed successfully, isolating the address-feature issue from the separate 10.0 PathBase regression. On 10.0.12, the held first request also demonstrated that separate 404 issue, but the address headers were recorded by middleware before routing.

This validates the harmful interleaving with real native request dispatch; it does **not** measure its production frequency. Full IIS/app-pool idle-timeout behavior was not tested.

### Exceptions (if any)

None from reading the address feature. The first request observes an empty collection rather than throwing.

### .NET Version

Reproduced with ASP.NET Core shared frameworks **10.0.12** and **9.0.20**, on Windows with x64 IIS Express. The diagnostic application was published using repository SDK **11.0.100-rc.1.26420.103**. ANCM was built from `dotnet/aspnetcore` commit `e523b3b676b8784e0bc8d6b1a574e5335c4ff3e5`.

For the native components used in the reproduction, after activating the repository .NET environment:

```powershell
. .\activate.ps1
.\eng\build.cmd -restore -BuildNative -projects src\Servers\IIS\AspNetCoreModuleV2\InProcessRequestHandler\InProcessRequestHandler.vcxproj /p:Platform=x64 /p:TargetArchitecture=x64
.\eng\build.cmd -BuildNative -projects src\Servers\IIS\AspNetCoreModuleV2\AspNetCore\AspNetCore.vcxproj /p:Platform=x64 /p:TargetArchitecture=x64
```

The IIS Express test configuration loaded the built `aspnetcorev2.dll` shim, and the published app included the built `aspnetcorev2_inprocess.dll`. No installed IIS configuration was modified.

### Anything else?

Related: [#69392](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/69392), a similar startup-initialization-ordering issue where the first in-process request can see an uninitialized `PathBase`. [#69398](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/69398) fixes that virtual-path initialization, but leaves address-feature publication after callback registration.

Unlike the PathBase regression, this address-feature behavior also reproduces on 9.0.20. It is tracked separately so the narrow 10.0 routing fix need not be blocked on it.

A likely fix is to publish `_serverAddressesFeature.Addresses` before registering native callbacks, while preserving the existing shutdown-safe handling of native application properties. This issue requests investigation/fix of the publication ordering, not new public API or permanent test-only hooks in product code.

