### Describe the bug

Setting either `KestrelServerLimits.KeepAliveTimeout` or `RequestHeadersTimeout` to the accepted value `Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan` causes its HTTP/1.1 timeout to expire on approximately the next heartbeat.

With only infinite keep-alive enabled, an idle TCP connection is closed with EOF after about one second. With only infinite request-header timeout enabled, sending an incomplete HTTP/1.1 header block results in HTTP 408 after about one second. The same connections remain open throughout the 2.5-second observation window with the finite defaults (130 seconds / 30 seconds).

The self-contained reproduction below uses only public APIs, binds to an ephemeral IPv4 localhost port, and requires no proxy, TLS, custom middleware, or third-party packages. Both settings are tested separately as well as together.

Reproduced with **Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 10.0.12 on Windows x64**, explicitly pinned with roll-forward disabled. The same EOF/408 behavior was also reproduced using 10.0.9.

### Expected Behavior

`Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan` should disable the corresponding timeout. An idle connection or incomplete header block should not time out on the next heartbeat when its corresponding limit is infinite.

For this bounded reproduction, all connections should remain open until the client's 2.5-second observation cancellation, except where a shorter finite timeout is explicitly configured (none here).

### Steps To Reproduce

With a .NET 10 SDK and the .NET/ASP.NET Core 10.0.12 runtimes installed, put these two files in an empty directory and run:

```text
dotnet run --configuration Release --project KestrelIssueRepro.csproj
```

**KestrelIssueRepro.csproj**

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>10.0.12</RuntimeFrameworkVersion>
    <RollForward>Disable</RollForward>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
```

**Program.cs**

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Server;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Server.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.Kestrel.Core;

Console.WriteLine($"Kestrel: {typeof(KestrelServerLimits).Assembly.Location}");
var observations = new List<object>();
foreach (var (name, keepAlive, headers) in new[]
{
    ("finite", TimeSpan.FromSeconds(130), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)),
    ("infinite keep-alive only", Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)),
    ("infinite headers only", TimeSpan.FromSeconds(130), Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan),
    ("both infinite", Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan)
})
{
    var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(Array.Empty<string>());
    builder.Logging.ClearProviders();
    builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
    {
        options.Listen(IPAddress.Loopback, 0, listen => listen.Protocols = HttpProtocols.Http1);
        options.Limits.KeepAliveTimeout = keepAlive;
        options.Limits.RequestHeadersTimeout = headers;
    });
    await using var app = builder.Build();
    app.MapGet("/", () => "ok");
    await app.StartAsync();
    var addresses = app.Services.GetRequiredService<IServer>().Features.Get<IServerAddressesFeature>()!;
    var address = new Uri(addresses.Addresses.Single());
    foreach (var partialHeaders in new[] { false, true })
    {
        using var client = new TcpClient();
        await client.ConnectAsync(IPAddress.Loopback, address.Port);
        using var stream = client.GetStream();
        if (partialHeaders)
            await stream.WriteAsync(Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes("GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost\r\n"));
        var watch = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        using var cancellation = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(2500));
        var buffer = new byte[512];
        try
        {
            var count = await stream.ReadAsync(buffer, cancellation.Token);
            observations.Add(new { Configuration = name, PartialHeaders = partialHeaders,
                Result = count == 0 ? "EOF" : "response", ElapsedMs = watch.ElapsedMilliseconds,
                Text = Encoding.ASCII.GetString(buffer, 0, count) });
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            observations.Add(new { Configuration = name, PartialHeaders = partialHeaders,
                Result = "still open after 2500ms", ElapsedMs = watch.ElapsedMilliseconds, Text = "" });
        }
    }
    await app.StopAsync();
}
Console.WriteLine(JsonSerializer.Serialize(observations, new JsonSerializerOptions { WriteIndented = true }));
```

The intentionally incomplete request is `GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost\r\n`: there is no terminating blank line. Each connection is observed independently for at most 2.5 seconds, then disposed.

### Actual Results

Observed with 10.0.12:

| Keep-alive limit | Header limit | Connect, send no bytes | Send incomplete headers |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 130 seconds | 30 seconds | Still open after 2,506 ms | Still open after 2,506 ms |
| Infinite | 30 seconds | EOF after 1,002 ms | Still open after 2,512 ms |
| 130 seconds | Infinite | Still open after 2,502 ms | HTTP 408 after 572 ms |
| Infinite | Infinite | EOF after 1,000 ms | HTTP 408 after 996 ms |

The exact sub-second/one-second timing depends on the heartbeat phase. The 408 response includes `Connection: close`.

With 10.0.9 and both limits infinite, an earlier run produced idle EOF after 764 ms and HTTP 408 for incomplete headers after 1,075 ms; the finite control remained open until its 2,500 ms observation limit.

### Exceptions (if any)

No application exception is needed to reproduce it. The observable server behavior is premature EOF or:

```http
HTTP/1.1 408 Request Timeout
Content-Length: 0
Connection: close
Server: Kestrel
```

### .NET Version

- SDK used to build the reproduction: **10.0.401**.
- .NET runtime and ASP.NET Core shared framework used for this run: **10.0.12**.
- Platform: **Windows x64**.
- Additional timeout-assignment probe: the installed ASP.NET Core **10.0.9 on Linux x64**.

### Anything else?

The source appears to explain the observed behavior:

1. The [10.0.12 limit setters](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/v10.0.12/src/Servers/Kestrel/Core/src/KestrelServerLimits.cs) explicitly accept `Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan` and store it as `TimeSpan.MaxValue`.
2. [TimeExtensions.ToTicks](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/v10.0.12/src/Servers/Kestrel/Core/src/Internal/Infrastructure/TimeExtensions.cs) maps `TimeSpan.MaxValue` to `long.MaxValue`.
3. [TimeoutControl.AssignTimeout](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/v10.0.12/src/Servers/Kestrel/Core/src/Internal/Infrastructure/TimeoutControl.cs) calculates:

```csharp
Interlocked.Exchange(
    ref _timeoutTimestamp,
    Interlocked.Read(ref _lastTimestamp) + timeoutTicks + _heartbeatIntervalTicks);
```

Adding a positive timestamp and heartbeat interval to `long.MaxValue` overflows to a negative deadline, which is already expired. A bounded reflection probe against the installed Linux 10.0.9 framework independently produced an infinite deadline of `-9221481969950958675` with a current timestamp of `1890065903817134` (timestamp frequency 1,000,000,000); finite 130-second and 30-second deadlines remained in the future.

I also checked the current `main` source: the setters, conversion, and assignment still contain the same calculation. This is not a claim that the problem was introduced in .NET 10; the same calculation exists in the 9.0.4 source.

